From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Darren Shepherd <darren@obot.ai>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 15:32:44 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] font+terminal: double and thick underline cursors

Two cursor shapes DECSCUSR has no name for, and which several terminals draw
anyway: two stacked underlines, and a thick underline filling the bottom quarter
of the cell - the shape the Windows console has drawn for decades and the one
Windows Terminal calls "vintage".

Both are sprite glyphs, so they are drawn once into the atlas by shared code and
every renderer gets them without touching a backend. The double one uses the
same arrangement as the double underline for *text*, so the cursor and an
underlined character line up. The thick one is measured from the bottom of the
cell rather than from the underline position, because it is a short block rather
than a heavy line and hanging it off the underline would put it somewhere
different in every font.

Both report as DECSCUSR 3 or 4, alongside block_hollow reporting as 1 or 2:
they are Ghostty styles, not new terminal protocol.

The C API's two cursor enums grew with them, which `zig build test` could not
have told me - it builds a module set that excludes them, and the library build
is what caught it. That is the second time today.

zig build test: 3096 passed, 58 skipped, 0 failed. The sprite test pins what
distinguishes each from a plain underline - the double is taller than one band,
the thick is a quarter of the cell - because either could silently degrade to
the single underline they replaced.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01BgDGqEqRBB8L5Ueoq1uuxy
